From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian Wiec <brian.wiec@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Jun 2025 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: vivid: force non-coherent CMA buffers for dma-contig
 allocator

When vivid uses the dma-contig allocator (allocators=1), its buffers
must be allocated from CMA and mapped uncached so that an external agent
reading DDR directly (e.g. ep_proxy's physical-address discovery via
/dev/mem) can observe CPU writes to the buffer.

On Versal, the default CMA pool lives at 0x800000000 (above 4GB).
The stock 32-bit DMA mask causes dma_alloc_coherent() to reject CMA
pages that fail the mask check, falling back to non-CMA upper DDR --
outside the region ep_proxy scans.  Additionally, the coherent
interconnect makes dma_alloc_coherent() return write-back cached
mappings, so CPU writes sit in cache and are invisible to uncached
/dev/mem reads.

Fix both issues:
  1. Widen the DMA mask to 64 bits so the high CMA pool is reachable.
  2. Set DMA_ATTR_FORCE_CONTIGUOUS on the vb2 queue to route
     allocations through CMA explicitly.
  3. Mark the device non-coherent so the DMA direct path produces
     uncached mappings that are visible to /dev/mem.

This matches the buffer properties of the real Xilinx VIPP/ISP
pipeline that ep_proxy was designed against.

Signed-off-by: Brian Wiec <brian.wiec@amd.com>
---
 drivers/media/test-drivers/vivid/vivid-core.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/test-drivers/vivid/vivid-core.c b/drivers/media/test-drivers/vivid/vivid-core.c
--- a/drivers/media/test-drivers/vivid/vivid-core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/test-drivers/vivid/vivid-core.c
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/font.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/dma-map-ops.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/videodev2.h>
 #include <linux/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
@@ -923,6 +924,10 @@
 	q->ops = ops;
 	q->mem_ops = allocators[dev->inst] == 1 ? &vb2_dma_contig_memops :
 						  &vb2_vmalloc_memops;
+	/* Route dma-contig allocations through CMA so buffers land in the
+	 * CMA window that ep_proxy scans for physical-address discovery. */
+	if (allocators[dev->inst] == 1)
+		q->dma_attrs |= DMA_ATTR_FORCE_CONTIGUOUS;
 	q->timestamp_flags = V4L2_BUF_FLAG_TIMESTAMP_MONOTONIC;
 	q->min_reqbufs_allocation = min_reqbufs_allocation;
 	q->lock = &dev->mutex;
@@ -1972,8 +1977,17 @@

 	spin_lock_init(&dev->cec_xfers_slock);

-	if (allocators[inst] == 1)
-		dma_coerce_mask_and_coherent(&pdev->dev, DMA_BIT_MASK(32));
+	if (allocators[inst] == 1) {
+		/* 64-bit mask so the high CMA pool (0x800000000) is reachable;
+		 * under the stock 32-bit mask, CMA pages fail the mask check
+		 * and the allocator falls back to non-CMA upper DDR. */
+		dma_coerce_mask_and_coherent(&pdev->dev, DMA_BIT_MASK(64));
+		/* Force non-coherent (uncached) buffers so an external agent
+		 * reading DDR directly (ep_proxy /dev/mem scan) sees CPU
+		 * writes.  Real VIPP/ISP pipeline buffers are uncached; vivid
+		 * defaults to coherent/cached, which breaks that assumption. */
+		pdev->dev.dma_coherent = false;
+	}

 	ret = vivid_create_queues(dev);
 	if (ret)
--
2.39.2

